{
  "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
  "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:17 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Adam G. Unikowski",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Mansell v. Mansell, this Court held that the Uniformed Services Former Spouses' Protection Act does not authorize State courts to treat waived Military Retirement Pay as divisible property in divorce. The question in this case is whether Mansell's holding applies only to waivers that preceded the divorce, or whether it applies to all waivers no matter when they occur, and we respectfully submit that the latter interpretation is correct."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Suppose that what the court had said is, taking a look at what the retirement pay is now, how much it is total, and had specified then a dollar amount for the wife equivalent to what the half was before the disability benefits, that would be okay; and if it takes disability later, she still keeps the amount that was awarded, the dollar amount."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: She can't go back into court -- I thought in divorce courts you can go back and change circumstances and seek a modification of the monetary awards. Why can't she do that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What is the general answer to that? I mean, it -- I gather there's a distinction between the finality of division of -- with respect to property, as opposed to some other -- as opposed to cash, I guess, or -- or income. Is -- is there that difference?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Wirken",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the -- I think the general answer, Your Honor, is that the a decree dividing -- to the extent it divides property is final. And for good reason. And in Arizona, certainly when there is a settlement agreement, as there was here, and it then becomes incorporated into the divorce decree, it's impossible, I would say, to go back and modify the division of property."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: So suppose the only asset is the pension. Then would the family court's order here be permissible?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Well, how do you deal with your adversary's position that her borrowing from our prior case that the only reasonable expectation that the Court or she could have had was that his military pay was always subject to a potential deduction for disability pay? That's basically his argument, that the act tells anyone relying on military pay that it can, as a matter of law, be changed by a disability award."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Why isn't your solution, and I think you're saying when he elects the disability, when -- when he does that, then the State court can provide an indemnification for her that's equal to the amount that she's losing. But isn't that just an end-run around the Mansell decision? The Mansell decision says --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Wirken",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I think it -- it's key that Congress gave the States the right to divide retirement pay at the time of divorce according to respective State law, and that when that happens, the divided interests are vested in each of the parties, and this order to indemnify does -- does not violate the Act. It does not preempt State law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: And then can the State court do something about that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Eisenstein (counsel)",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. EISENSTEIN: It's different because there are other assets besides the military -- the disability benefits used to satisfy the indemnification order in this case, and so there's a very --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What does -- what does that have to do with my question?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: I think the question is can a State depart -- a -- a State court depart from the rule that the State court would use in dividing property"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Eisenstein (counsel)",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. EISENSTEIN: Well -- well, your Honor, we don't -- I don't interpret your Honor's hypothetical as a division of property."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Eisenstein (counsel)",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e75f3c88285-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. EISENSTEIN: No, your Honor. I think that the court can use its broadly applicable rules for enforcing existing property divisions; in this case, the Arizona rule is one that prohibits a party from unilaterally harming the interest of the other spouse, and gives a makeup remedy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1031",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
